<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tomás de Torquemada</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1420">1420</a>–<a href="/years/1498">1498</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Thomas of Torquemada</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tomás de Torquemada was a Spanish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-order">Dominican friar</a> and the first Grand Inquisitor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-inquisition">Spanish Inquisition</a>. Prior of the monastery of Santa Cruz at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/segovia">Segovia</a>, he became confessor to Isabella I and later to Ferdinand, and was named Grand Inquisitor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> a year after his appointment as an inquisitor in 1482. Under him the Inquisition grew from a single tribunal at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> to a network of two dozen, and he was one of the chief supporters of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alhambra">Alhambra</a> Decree of 1492, which expelled the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> from Spain. Because of the Inquisition's use of torture and burning at the stake, which he approved, his name became synonymous with cruelty and religious intolerance.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tomas-de-torquemada.jpg" alt="Tomás de Torquemada" width="189" height="292" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maestro de la Virgen de los Reyes Católicos. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tomás_de_Torquemada.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Torquemada was born on 14 October 1420, either in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid">Valladolid</a> or in the nearby village of Torquemada. The chronicler Hernando del Pulgar, himself a converso, recorded that Tomás's uncle Juan de Torquemada, a theologian and cardinal, was of converso descent. Pulgar is considered to have made the claim out of hatred for the nephew, and a 2020 study of Juan's ancestors found no Jewish converts. Tomás entered the San Pablo Dominican monastery very young and earned a reputation for learning, piety and austerity. He met the young Princess Isabella, and the two established a religious and ideological rapport. He was present at her coronation in 1474.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The Crown of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a> had Dominican inquisitors almost continuously through much of the 14th and 15th centuries. Ferdinand and Isabella petitioned <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sixtus-iv">Pope Sixtus IV</a> for a Holy Office to administer an inquisition in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and he established it in late 1478. The bull gave the sovereigns full powers to name inquisitors, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> kept the right to appoint the nominees formally.</p><p>In 1484 Torquemada gave up his role as royal confessor to Diego Deza, who later succeeded him as Grand Inquisitor. The next year, at a general assembly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a>, he promulgated twenty-eight articles of faith to guide the inquisitors. Under the Alhambra Decree of 31 March 1492 about 40,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> were expelled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, and about 50,000 others received baptism to remain. Many kept some Jewish traditions in secret and were among the chief targets of the Inquisition. So many clemency petitions reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> that the Pope called the Inquisition's representatives to Rome three times.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>On 23 June 1494 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-alexander-vi">Pope Alexander VI</a> appointed four assistant inquisitors, officially because of Torquemada's failing health, though many historians believe that complaints about his zeal were the real cause. He retired to the monastery of St. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-aquinas">Thomas Aquinas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avila">Ávila</a>, where in 1498 he held his last general assembly, setting rules to curb some administrative abuses. He died there on 16 September 1498. His tomb was ransacked in 1832, two years before the Inquisition was disbanded, and his bones were allegedly stolen and burned in the manner of an auto-da-fé.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tomás de Torquemada” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
